---
import PathwayLandingLayout from '../../layouts/PathwayLandingLayout.astro';
import { getPathwayHero } from '@/lib/pathwayHeroes';
import { SITE_CONFIG } from '@/lib/seo/config';

const title = 'EB-5 for Startup Founders | StudentEB5';
const metaDescription =
  'EB-5 for startup founders explains why founders often need immigration status independent of a company they control, and how EB-5’s investor-status independence can enter that evaluation.';
const canonicalUrl = `${SITE_CONFIG.url}/pathways/eb5-for-startup-founders`;
const h1 = 'EB-5 for Startup Founders';
const subhead =
  'Why some founders evaluate EB-5 when staying in the U.S. cannot depend solely on status tied to a company they themselves control.';

const relatedResearch = [
  {
    title: 'EB-5 Investment Process',
    url: '/eb5-investment-process',
    description: 'Step-by-step filing sequence from source of funds through I-829.',
  },
];
const hero = getPathwayHero('eb5-for-startup-founders');

---

<PathwayLandingLayout
  title={title}
  metaDescription={metaDescription}
  canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
  h1={h1}
  subhead={subhead}
  heroImage={hero.image}
  heroImageWebp={hero.webp}
  heroImageAlt="EB-5 pathway for startup founders"
  relatedResearch={relatedResearch}
  secondaryCtaText="Talk to Someone About EB-5 for Startup Founders"
  breadcrumbTitle="EB-5 for Startup Founders"
  keywords={[
    'EB-5 for startup founders',
    'founder immigration EB-5',
    'startup founder green card',
    'self-sponsored founder EB-5',
  ]}
>
  <h2>Founders are not just “entrepreneurs on H-1B”</h2>
  <p>
    Startup founders often sit in a different immigration bind than salaried employees. The company they are building may
    be the very entity that would need to sponsor them, which can create circular or fragile status arrangements,
    especially early when payroll, ownership, and control are still taking shape. Options that depend on an arm's-length
    employer or on lottery selection can be a poor fit for someone whose job is to run their own venture.
  </p>

  <h2>Why investor-status independence matters</h2>
  <p>
    EB-5's structure is built around a qualifying investment and petition, not around continued employment by a third-party
    sponsor. For some founders, that independence is the point: immigration planning can proceed on a track that is not
    identical to the startup's financing rounds, board decisions, or ability to file an H-1B. EB-5 still requires capital,
    lawful source-of-funds documentation, project or enterprise diligence, and USCIS adjudication. It does not guarantee
    status outcomes or solve every founder visa problem. Filing sequence and process detail:
    <a href="/eb5-investment-process">EB-5 investment process</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What this page is not</h2>
  <p>
    Established business owners evaluating EB-5 primarily around operating-company income and source-of-funds
    documentation should also see
    <a href="/pathways/eb5-for-entrepreneurs">EB-5 for entrepreneurs &amp; business owners</a>. Founders who are mainly
    reacting to H-1B layoff risk in a salaried tech role may find a closer fit on
    <a href="/pathways/eb5-for-tech-workers">EB-5 for tech workers</a>.
  </p>
</PathwayLandingLayout>
